      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine how many distinct equilateral triangles exist in the plane of a nine-sided regular polygon \\( A_1A_2A_3A_4A_5A_6A_7A_8A_9 \\) that have at least two vertices from the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\).\n\nConsider the properties of a regular nine-sided polygon. The vertices of the polygon can be labeled as \\( A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9 \\) in a cyclic order. For an equilateral triangle to be formed, the distance between any two vertices must be the same. This distance is the same as the side length of the polygon, which is the same as the distance between any two adjacent vertices.\n\nLet's label the vertices of the polygon as follows:\n\\[ A_1, A_2, A_3, A_4, A_5, A_6, A_7, A_8, A_9. \\]\n\nWe need to find all possible equilateral triangles that can be formed with at least two vertices from this set. The vertices of an equilateral triangle must be equally spaced around the polygon. Let's consider the possible configurations:\n\n1. Triangles with vertices \\( A_1, A_4, A_7 \\)\n2. Triangles with vertices \\( A_2, A_5, A_8 \\)\n3. Triangles with vertices \\( A_3, A_6, A_9 \\)\n4. Triangles with vertices \\( A_1, A_2, A_3 \\)\n5. Triangles with vertices \\( A_4, A_5, A_6 \\)\n6. Triangles with vertices \\( A_7, A_8, A_9 \\)\n7. Triangles with vertices \\( A_1, A_5, A_9 \\)\n8. Triangles with vertices \\( A_2, A_6, A_1 \\)\n9. Triangles with vertices \\( A_3, A_7, A_2 \\)\n10. Triangles with vertices \\( A_4, A_8, A_3 \\)\n11. Triangles with vertices \\( A_9, A_1, A_4 \\)\n12. Triangles with vertices \\( A_1, A_3, A_5 \\)\n13. Triangles with vertices \\( A_2, A_4, A_6 \\)\n14. Triangles with vertices \\( A_3, A_5, A_7 \\)\n15. Triangles with vertices \\( A_4, A_6, A_8 \\)\n16. Triangles with vertices \\( A_5, A_7, A_9 \\)\n17. Triangles with vertices \\( A_6, A_8, A_1 \\)\n18. Triangles with vertices \\( A_7, A_9, A_2 \\)\n19. Triangles with vertices \\( A_8, A_1, A_3 \\)\n20. Triangles with vertices \\( A_9, A_2, A_4 \\)\n21. Triangles with vertices \\( A_1, A_3, A_5 \\)\n22. Triangles with vertices \\( A_2, A_4, A_6 \\)\n23. Triangles with vertices \\( A_3, A_5, A_7 \\)\n24. Triangles with vertices \\( A_4, A_6, A_8 \\)\n25. Triangles with vertices \\( A_5, A_7, A_9 \\)\n26. Triangles with vertices \\( A_6, A_8, A_1 \\)\n27. Triangles with vertices \\( A_7, A_9, A_2 \\)\n28. Triangles with vertices \\( A_8, A_1, A_3 \\)\n29. Triangles with vertices \\( A_9, A_2, A_4 \\)\n30. Triangles with vertices \\( A_1, A_3, A_5 \\)\n31. Triangles with vertices \\( A_2, A_4, A_6 \\)\n32. Triangles with vertices \\( A_3, A_5, A_7 \\)\n33. Triangles with vertices \\( A_4, A_6, A_8 \\)\n34. Triangles with vertices \\( A_5, A_7, A_9 \\)\n35. Triangles with vertices \\( A_6, A_8, A_9 \\)\n\nAfter removing the duplicate equilateral triangles, we find that there are 12 distinct equilateral triangles. Therefore, the number of distinct equilateral triangles is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
